{
  "title": "Premise Order Matters in Reasoning with Large Language Models",
  "authors": [
    "Xinyun Chen",
    "Ryan A. Chi",
    "Xuezhi Wang",
    "Denny Zhou"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2402.08939",
  "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are surprisingly brittle to the ordering of premises in reasoning tasks, despite the fact that such ordering does not alter the underlying task. This work investigates the effect of premise order on LLM reasoning, demonstrating that LLMs achieve the best performance when the premise order aligns with the context required in intermediate reasoning steps.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The **premise order effect** refers to the significant impact of the ordering of premises on the reasoning performance of large language models (LLMs). Despite the fact that the premise order does not change the underlying task, LLMs are more comfortable reasoning via reading left-to-right instead of back-and-forth, which can be attributed to the auto-regressive model design or the reasoning bias learned from the training corpus.",
